52.\par
\par
Over and Done With.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
It was late afternoon when Marquess Riette von Lindman arrived at the Herhardt mansion. His carriage was followed by another carriage packed\par
with luggage for his stay at Arvis.\par
\ldblquote Welcome, Riette,\rdblquote  Elise von Herhardt said, smiling brightly. \ldblquote I wish my sister could have come along with you.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Ah, yes,\rdblquote  he replied. \ldblquote Mother is about to leave for a trip.\rdblquote  \ldblquote Is she going south for the winter again?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Yes, she can\rquote t stand the cold,\rdblquote  he replied in a gentle tone. \ldblquote Where is Dowager Herhardt?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote You can greet her later. She\rquote s taking a nap right now.\rdblquote\par
Elise led Riette to the drawing room. As Matthias\rquote s cousin and friend, Riette had visited Arvis countless times over the years. The Herhardts had always treated him like part of the family.\par
\par
After having tea with his aunt, he went out for a leisurely stroll in the forest. Matthias wasn\rquote t going to be home until night, and it was going to be a while until the servants had finished unloading his luggage and preparing his room. More than anything, he was curious about the girl who lived by the forest, the girl who was jeopardizing Matthias and Claudine\rquote s perfect life- Layla.\par
Of course, Riette had long been aware of Layla, but had never paid her much mind. He had just lumped her in with the rest of Arvis\rquote s staff. He had pitied her for her disadvantaged background, and he had known that she was quite pretty. That was all. He was thus quite surprised when he found out that she was the woman that Matthias had taken an interest in.\par
Scandals involving affairs between aristocrats and their maids were common and usually attracted little attention. It would be a different story, however, if the aristocrat in question was Duke Herhardt, even if it proved to be nothing more than a passing fancy.\par
\ldblquote Duke Herhardt lied. \rdblquote\par
As the gardener\rquote s cottage appeared in the distance, something popped into Riette\rquote s mind that Claudine had told him one evening late the previous summer.\par
\ldblquote He lied in order to separate Layla and the doctor\rquote s son. \rdblquote\par
After saying this, Claudine had slowly turned towards Riette and smiled.\par
Or rather, her lips had smiled; her eyes had remained cold and emotionless. \ldblquote Matthias von Herhardt lied and schemed so that he could have that lowly\par
orphan for himself. Can you believe it, Riette?\rdblquote  she had asked, giggling.\par
Riette had asked her what sort of lies Matthias had told, but she hadn\rquote t elaborated.\par
\par
\ldblquote I hope he gets her as soon as possible, \rdblquote  she had said, calmly gazing out the window at the sun sinking below the horizon after a long summer day at the Brandt estate. \ldblquote The sooner he has her, the sooner he\rquote ll be done with her, \rquote\rquote she had quietly added, her face resembling that of a bored spectator at a third-rate stage performance.\par
She had never shown the slightest hint of jealousy towards Layla. She had displayed a slightly derisive attitude towards her noble fiance\rquote s taste, as if she were disappointed he hadn\rquote t set his sights a bit higher, but that was it. And Riette knew that this was how she really felt.\par
If Claudine had instead seemed jealous or unhappy about the situation, Riette might have had the courage to tell her not to get stuck in a marriage that was going to make her unhappy when she could marry someone else who would make her happy \emdash Riette himself. But she had continued to see Matthias as the key to her future happiness. And Riette was well aware of this as well.\par
That day, the two of them had enjoyed light conversation over tea. Dinner had been similarly pleasant and carefree. The next morning, Riette had left the Brandt estate. Claudine had remained standing in the doorway until his car had drifted out of sight.\par
As he reflected on this memory, Riette wondered why it had suddenly arisen in his mind. He let out a hollow laugh.\par
Just then, he arrived at the cottage. He noticed that the cottage was linked to the unfenced hunting ground by the forest path.\par
The sun had already begun to set, but the inside of the cottage was dark. Reluctant to turn back around right away after walking all that way for nothing, he decided to wait for a bit. He leaned on the porch\rquote s railing and looked up at the trees, which had dropped about half their leaves.\par
\par
Riette loved a woman who was never going to let love make her unhappy.\par
Perhaps this was the reason for his own unhappiness.\par
He accepted this fate. He loved his simple, carefree life, and he wasn\rquote t above carrying out an even more simple, carefree bit of mischief, especially if it would bring him entertainment and bring Claudine happiness.\par
Time drew on. Just as he was lighting a cigarette, he heard the sound of footsteps approaching on the forest path. Soon he could also hear the sound of two people talking.\par
Gazing in the direction of the sounds, he took a long drag of his cigarette and slowly exhaled the smoke. Before long, he saw a man as big as a bear emerge from the forest next to a woman scarcely half his size. They stopped at the edge of the cottage\rquote s front yard when they saw him.\par
He smiled and started walking towards them. The gardener gave him a terse greeting once he recognized him, and the woman bowed her head.\par
\ldblquote Hello, you must be the bird girl who lives by the forest,\rdblquote  Riette said with the same calm attitude he had shown when he had encountered her crying in the forest on her way to bury the birds Matthias had killed. \ldblquote Ah, I suppose you\rquote re not a bird girl anymore. Now you\rquote re a bird lady. Or would you prefer I call you a bird teacher?\rdblquote\par
There was a doubtful look in her bespectacled eyes. She frowned as she listened to him prattle on.\par
His impression of her remained unchanged. She was pretty, but she wasn\rquote t a stunning beauty capable of capturing every man\rquote s heart. Nonetheless, he could vaguely sense that there was something special about her, something that had bewitched both his cousin and the doctor\rquote s son.\par
\ldblquote Well anyway, nice to meet you, Layla.\rdblquote\par
\par
