\ldblquote Be careful of Marquess Lindman,\rdblquote  Bill said in a serious tone as he sat at the dinner table.\par
Layla chuckled as she set a pot of freshly cooked stew down in the middle of the table.\par
\ldblquote Come now, this isn\rquote t a laughing matter,\rdblquote  he said. \ldblquote Uncle Bill\'85\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Do you think that idler really came all the way over here on a leisurely stroll? No way. He was definitely up to something.\rdblquote  He tore his slice of bread in half roughly. \ldblquote He went right up to you and started blathering on about this and that. I\rquote m just saying, it was suspicious. Be careful.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Yes, Uncle Bill,\rdblquote  she replied, knowing that agreeing was the only way to get him to stop harping on this issue.\par
He frowned, sensing that she was just humoring him. \ldblquote You should take my words to heart, Layla. Not all aristocrats are as decent and respectable as Duke Herhardt.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Yes, I kn\'85 huh?\rdblquote  She furrowed her brow. She wanted to dispute his characterization of the duke, but she couldn\rquote t. He had described Duke Herhardt the way everyone knew him, besides her.\par
She nodded reluctantly, keeping her feelings to herself, and took a quick bite of her bread. As she chewed, her memories of that indecent, unrespectable man only intensified. As she thought of the mocking way he had bowed to her,\par
\par
and the excessively beautiful pen he had sent to the school, the bread went down the wrong pipe and she started coughing violently.\par
\ldblquote What\rquote s the matter?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Nothing,\rdblquote  she said, shaking her head. \ldblquote I was just so hungry I ate too fast.\rdblquote  She took off her glasses and wiped away the tears that the coughing had caused.\par
Bill stared at her for a moment, then burst into laughter. \ldblquote At times like these, you still seem like a child,\rdblquote  he gently chided. \ldblquote Eat a lot, but take your time.\rdblquote  He took a sizable hunk of meat from the pot and placed it in her bowl. Then another. Soon there was a pile of meat rising from her bowl.\par
\ldblquote That\rquote s too much!\rdblquote  she shouted.\par
He was stubborn as always. \ldblquote Come now, Layla, I like kids who can eat like a cow.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I\rquote m not a kid anymore,\rdblquote  she protested.\par
In response, he placed one last piece of meat on top of the pile.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
They spent the rest of their dinner laughing and chatting. Layla wasn\rquote t able to eat as much as a cow, but she managed to eat enough to make Bill happy.\par
After the meal, he fixed her creaky desk chair while she cleaned up the kitchen. Then she poured two large mugs of coffee and they sat on the porch, gazing at the leaves falling in the forest. The weather had already started to get cold, but they still finished each day chatting on the porch, as they did every year until winter rolled around.\par
\par
After that, they got ready for bed. Pausing before he went into his room, he said, \ldblquote Sleep well, Layla.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote You too, Uncle Bill,\rdblquote  she replied. A sliver of light from outside shone through a gap in the curtain, illuminating the gentle smile on her face. \ldblquote Good night.\rdblquote\par
She went to her room, sat in the chair he had just fixed for her, and graded her students\rquote  quizzes. She then read a few chapters of a mystery novel she had borrowed from the library and wrote replies to letters from some fellow teachers she had made friends with.\par
As she finished the last reply and capped the old, worn-out pen she was using, she was reminded of the new pen the duke had sent her.\par
She stared into space for a moment, then resolutely opened the desk\rquote s drawer. She had reluctantly brought the pen home. There it was, still in its box.\par
She thought of the dismissive things people had always used to say about her when she had made small mistakes as a child.\par
\ldblquote I knew it.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote What can you expect from a child raised without parents?\rdblquote\par
These comments had always been said with a mixture of pity and contempt.\par
At first, she had been perplexed by the fact that she was reprimanded more than her peers for making the same mistakes as they did, or even when she was much more well-behaved than they were. But as she grew up, she came to understand that life wasn\rquote t fair. There were double standards wherever one looked.\par
This made her want to be even better. Even if she could never be perfect, she wanted at least to live a life that others didn\rquote t pity her for. Her resolve was\par
\par
strengthened each time she faced another cruel double standard. She felt she owed this to Uncle Bill, who had so lovingly raised her.\par
With these thoughts in mind, she took the box with the pen in it out of the drawer and grabbed a piece of brown paper.\par
After meticulously wrapping the box in the paper, she carefully wrote an address on it, using the same old, worn-out pen she had written the letters with. The recipient was the lord of Arvis, Duke Matthias von Herhardt. For the return address, she wrote down the same unfamiliar name and address that had been on the original package she had received at the school.\par
The next morning, at the crack of dawn, she put the box in her bag and set off for work. She had to leave earlier than usual so that she could stop by the neighboring village\rquote s post office on her way.\par
After returning the gift, she felt like a weight had been lifted off her chest.\par
She believed that it was all over and done with now. She was sure of it.\par
\par
