53.\par
\par
That Bird\rquote s Owner.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\ldblquote This pains my heart every time I see it, Matthias,\rdblquote  Riette said, clicking his tongue as he opened Matthias\rquote s liquor cabinet.\par
Matthias wasn\rquote t much of a drinker, but the liquor cabinet in his room was always fully stocked with the finest spirits.\par
\ldblquote It\rquote s not right that you should neglect these precious bottles,\rdblquote  Riette said with a bold grin as he took a bottle to the table.\par
As always, Matthias seemed not to care what his cousin was doing.\par
The music coming from the phonograph blended pleasingly with the crackling of the logs in the fireplace. Riette filled a liquor glass that a servant had brought and held it out to Matthias. The graceful motion with which Matthias accepted the glass resembled the piano melody flowing through the room.\par
\par
Riette leaned so far back into the sofa that he was practically lying down and looked closely at Matthias as if he were observing a stranger. Matthias was lost in thought, staring at the rim of his glass. The little canary, which now felt like it had become part of the room, was cheerfully hopping about next to him.\par
As Riette continued to look at Matthias, he recalled something someone had said about him at a social club: \ldblquote Your cousin is a gentlemanly demon.\rdblquote\par
Matthias had never spoken to him about his time serving as a captain on the overseas war front. Rather than being from humbleness, this seemed to be more because he attached no significance to his service. It was instead from the mouths of other aristocratic officers that had served alongside him that word of his heroic exploits had spread.\par
Riette hadn\rquote t served in that war, but from their descriptions, he felt like he had seen Matthias on the battlefield with his own eyes. He had a peculiar attitude\emdash unlike other young soldiers, he was neither a passionate fighter intoxicated by the thrill of battle nor was he utterly bored by the tedium of military duties. He systematically carried out the tasks assigned to him, and though he didn\rquote t deny the satisfaction of accomplishment, he didn\rquote t seem to think any of it meant much, either. Anyone talking about Captain Herhardt inevitably concluded their account by saying, \ldblquote So\'85 I really don\rquote t understand him.\rdblquote\par
Riette knew better than anyone that this dispirited comment summed up Matthias von Herhardt better than any other description.\par
\ldblquote I don\rquote t understand him, \rquote\rquote always uttered with a sigh.\par
Riette sighed as he thought about it now. I don\rquote t understand him.\par
A lifetime of observing Matthias had brought him to that same conclusion. Just as combining all the beautiful colors of the rainbow produced pure white\par
\par
light, so all of Matthias\rquote s various qualities combined to produce an inscrutable whole.\par
He was a noble aristocrat, a worthy successor, a good cousin, and an honorable soldier. Each of these qualities was clear and distinct in itself, yet the man to which they added up was impossible to parse.\par
Some praised Duke Herhardt for the way he suppressed his personal desires so that he could fulfill his duties, but Riette was skeptical of this. The Matthias he knew had no desires to suppress. And this made his attitude towards this Layla Llewellyn girl all the more perplexing.\par
Riette smiled, his lips wet with the drink. Matthias looked at him. The canary was now sitting on his shoulder. One might have thought this would bother Matthias, but he didn\rquote t seem to mind it at all.\par
\ldblquote How long do you think you\rquote ll continue to act as that bird\rquote s owner?\rdblquote  Riette asked.\par
\ldblquote As long as I want to,\rdblquote  Matthias replied without hesitation. He then finally took a sip of the liquor glass he had been holding. The bird flew up into the air briefly before landing back on his shoulder and rubbing its beak on the strap of his suspenders.\par
\ldblquote How long do you think that will be?\rdblquote  Riette asked.\par
\ldblquote Who knows.\rdblquote  Matthias set down his glass and leaned against the armrest of his seat. His onyx cufflinks reflected the light from the fireplace.\par
\ldblquote Aren\rquote t you curious about anything?\rdblquote  Riette asked. \ldblquote Why I\rquote m here at Arvis, what I\rquote m planning to do here, anything like that?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Nope,\rdblquote  Matthias indifferently replied. Riette had appeared at Arvis out of the blue and stayed for weeks before. It was nothing out of the ordinary.\par
\ldblquote Fine, be that way,\rdblquote  Riette replied, laughing loudly before he drained his glass. He looked closely at Matthias and thought, Is it lust? That seemed like the\par
\par
most likely explanation. It\rquote s just a man s lust for a pretty woman. It\rquote s nature. Even someone without any emotions would still have natural instincts. But why that orphan, of all people? He\rquote s always been immune to the charms of women much more beautiful and elegant than her. Soon he felt like his thoughts were going in circles, so he decided to stop pondering the matter.\par
The bird was hopping about on the table now. Matthias gave a short whistle and it slew right back to him.\par
