Hmm, she\rquote s heading to work on foot today. Matthias grinned as he saw someone far in the distance, past the mansion\rquote s large rose garden, walking along the path from the hunting ground. Even at such a distance, he knew who it was at once-Layla.\par
He stepped closer to the window. Leaning against the window frame, which was covered by damask curtains that extended all the way from the high ceiling to the floor, he gazed at her as she approached. He gave a short whistle, and his bird immediately flew to him and landed on his hand.\par
He moved his hand closer to the window as if to show his canary the woman walking outside. However, the bird seemed uninterested and flew back to its cage. As Layla continued walking, she was soon no longer visible from his bedroom window. But Matthias stayed by the window for a long while.\par
At some point recently, he had made a habit of starting each day by that window, basking in the sunlight and watching Layla as she headed off for work.\par
I wonder if she\rquote s all healed by now, he thought, unwittingly furrowing his brow at the memory of her tiny, bruised body.\par
He had not spoken to her since that night. He could have visited her and seen for himself how she was doing, but he didn\rquote t want to do that. It was strange. He didn\rquote t fully understand where his hesitance was coming from.\par
He left the window, sat in the easy chair by the fireplace, and picked up the newspaper that he had been reading earlier. However, he couldn\rquote t focus-the\par
\par
words on the page seemed meaningless to him.\par
That night, Layla had cried. He had seen her cry countless times before, and yet somehow, this time had felt different, as if he were witnessing it for the first time. He couldn\rquote t get it out of his thoughts.\par
\ldblquote Wait\rdblquote he had said, stopping her before she could get off the bed. He had held a small bottle of medicine out to her. \ldblquote This is a painkiller.\rdblquote\par
She had simply looked up at his face with a dazed look in her eyes.\par
He had removed the bottle\rquote s cap and stepped closer to her. Then he had grabbed her chin and made her open her mouth. She had squirmed, but he had still managed to pour some of the medicine down her throat. It was quite bitter, but she had maintained a serene expression as she swallowed it.\par
He had chuckled, then retrieved his handkerchief and wiped her lips dry. And then he had taken a piece of candy that he had put in the first-aid kid, and placed it in her mouth. She had stared at him for a moment, then after the candy had started to melt in her mouth, tears had started to trickle down from her eyes.\par
The unexpected tears had caught him off guard. Without making any sound, she had continued to shed thick tears. He had wondered what she was so upset about as he watched her holding the piece of candy in her mouth, neither spitting it out nor swallowing it.\par
He had then stiffly placed his hand against her face and wiped her tears.\par
But she had just kept crying.\par
Unsure what to do, he had asked, \ldblquote Are you still in pain?\rdblquote  She had nodded in response.\par
Not knowing what to say, he had simply hugged her. Having previously tried to push him away, she had now resignedly let herself be pulled into his embrace.\par
\par
He had held her for a long time, as she sobbed and chewed the candy. By the time she stopped crying, her hot, sad tears had soaked the front of his sweater. Whenever he reflected on that memory, the somber emotions he had felt that night swept over him again, just as they were doing right now.\par
Without reading a single word, he put the newspaper back down and slumped deep into the chair, looking upward. As he stared at the ceiling, he could see her crying face again.\par
Usually, seeing her cry made him happy.\par
Previously, he had enjoyed seeing her tear-soaked face so much, he had eagerly made her cry whenever he had the chance. Of course, there had been times that he hadn\rquote t enjoyed it so much, such as when someone or something other than himself had been the cause of her tears. At those times, he had disliked the sight and wanted her crying to stop.\par
It had always been clear-cut like that for him. And not just with her tears- he had felt the same way about everything of Layla\rquote s. In fact, he had felt the same way about everything in his life. It all had to be his.\par
He reflected on his time with Layla that night like a chess master analyzing a game he had lost to see what went wrong.\par
They were strange tears, he thought. Usually, she would cry when she was overcome with anger, shame, or fear. He had no problem enjoying her crying on such occasions.\par
But that night, she had seemed almost as if she wasn\rquote t aware that she was crying. Her eyes had contained no anger or fear, and yet they hadn\rquote t been blank, either. They had been full of some feeling he couldn\rquote t recognize, even upon reflection.\par
He had wanted to stop her crying, but not in the angry way he had wanted to stop her when she had been crying over Kyle Ettman.\par
\par
The feeling inside him was strange and uncomfortable, but he didn\rquote t especially mind it. It was almost like the feeling of being mildly intoxicated by a fine liqueur. He felt listless and yet slightly agitated as if he were being chased by something. He felt both confused and happy at the same time.\par
Happy. Yes, I feet happy. He felt happy in a way that was wholly distinct from the pleasure he had previously derived from making Layla cry. He started to wonder if perhaps that was the reason he kept her around instead of casting her away so that he could feel this happiness. So that he could have this happiness for himself.\par
He thought back to the moment after she had stopped crying when he had gazed at her tired face. She had calmly let him wipe away her tears and sweep her hair aside. But like a faintly sparkling light that finally went out, that strange emotion in her eyes had faded away, leaving only fear and resignation in its place. He had kept gazing at her, wondering if the emotion would return, but it hadn\rquote t.\par
He wanted to recover the happiness he had felt that night. But no matter how many times he ran through the memory, he couldn\rquote t feel it. He frowned. It was as if the feeling were sand slipping through his fingers. Just then, there was a polite knock at his door.\par
\ldblquote Your Grace, it\rquote s Hessen.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Come in,\rdblquote  Matthias replied, lowering his gaze from the ceiling.\par
Hessen walked over to him and stood next to his chair. \ldblquote Lady Herhardt inquired about your plans for this evening.\par
\ldblquote Mother did?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Yes. She had planned to go to tonight\rquote s benefit performance at the public hall with Dowager Herhardt, but Lady Herhardt is coming down with a cold, so she will have to stay in. Thus, if you had no other plans for this evening, she\par
\par
would like you to accompany your grandmother in her stead. Will that work for you. Your Grace?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote A benefit performance?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Yes, students from several schools in the Karlsbar area will be performing, including the local village school.\rquote\par
Suddenly, Matthias understood why Layla had been walking to work. Now that he thought about it, she seemed to have been dressed a bit more stylishly than usual, as well.\par
\ldblquote Sure,\rdblquote  he replied. I\rquote ll go.\rdblquote\par
