\ldblquote You\rquote ve been looking quite lively lately, Matthias,\rdblquote  Dowager Herhardt cheerfully said as she gazed at her grandson\rquote s face. \ldblquote Do you have some good news to tell me about?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote No, not that I can think of,\rdblquote  he replied with a polite smile.\par
\par
Despite his disappointing response, his grandmother knew that there had to be something behind his uncharacteristically bright mood. She realized with amusement that he was finally acting his age. He was acting like a young man at the height of his vitality instead of acting many years older like he usually did. Sometimes she forgot how young he still was, but it was impossible to miss right now.\par
\ldblquote Well, anyway, I\rquote m happy to see you in such a good mood,\rdblquote  she said, deciding not to press him any further on the matter. \ldblquote Now, you may think of events like this as trivial, but they are one of the most important ways to gain public support and respect.\rdblquote  She looked around the auditorium, then looked at her grandson again. \ldblquote I think it\rquote s wonderful that as head of the household, you\rquote re taking the trouble to come tonight. Your grandfather and father were never this considerate.\rdblquote\par
The public liked to refer to Matthias as the masterpiece of the House of Herhardt. Although Dowager Herhardt would always giggle and say that this was overpraised, deep down, she believed it more than anyone.\par
It was almost as if she had lived such a long life, outliving her husband and her son, just so that she could be around for the glorious days when her grandson was the head of the household. This thought had been popping into her mind occasionally.\par
As she gazed lovingly at her grandson, the auditorium\rquote s lights went down.\par
The audience fell silent, and the emcee appeared on the stage.\par
Matthias sat with perfect posture and clapped politely along with the rest of the audience. However, he had absolutely no interest in what was happening on the stage. The only thing on his mind was the necklace that Layla was wearing.\par
\par
He hadn\rquote t been sure at first when he had seen her from afar, but when she had come closer, it had been clear that it was the emerald necklace he had gotten for her. To him, that necklace was like a sign indicating that she was his. Even if she didn\rquote t know it, he did, and that gave him a deep feeling of satisfaction.\par
His attention was finally drawn to the stage when the children from the village school started performing a play. Seeing many familiar faces, he looked closely and realized that they were the children who had visited Arvis with Layla.\par
He shifted his gaze to the unlit area in front of the stage. Sure enough, there was Layla, watching her students. The nervous children kept glancing at their teacher throughout the performance. The smallest child among them, whom Matthias recognized as the little girl who had spilled ice cream on Layla\rquote s clothes on the day of the field trip, looked ready to cry. He wondered what expression was on Miss Llewellyn\rquote s face. From behind, she looked just as nervous as her students.\par
The play was about fairies living in a forest. The children traversed the stage wearing translucent wings, occasionally eliciting delighted laughter from the audience. But Matthias\rquote s eyes remained fixed on the young woman nervously standing in front of the stage. He was thus more startled than others in the audience when one of the children suddenly started crying loudly.\par
\ldblquote Oh dear,\rdblquote  Dowager Herhardt muttered.\par
Following his grandmother\rquote s gaze, he saw that it was the little girl. \ldblquote Miss Llewellyn!!\rdblquote  she wailed, looking straight at Layla.\par
Now that the play had been interrupted, a look of panic fell over the other children\rquote s faces.\par
\par
Matthias narrowed his eyes as he looked at Layla. Will she be able to handle this?\par
The principal rushed over to Layla and whispered something in her ear. She reacted with a startled expression and shook her head, then gave in and quickly walked up onto the stage. The entire audience watched as the young teacher comforted the crying child.\par
\ldblquote Oh my, it\rquote s her,\rdblquote  Dowager Herhardt said, smiling as she recognized the teacher. \ldblquote It\rquote s Bill Remmer\rquote s adopted daughter.\rdblquote\par
Layla was crouched behind a fake flower bush, consoling the little girl. Eventually, the girl stopped crying, but she didn\rquote t seem at all capable of resuming her role in the play. Furthermore, some of the other children appeared to be ready to cry as well if the situation weren\rquote t resolved quickly.\par
Finally, the principal seemed to decide that special measures were called for. He summoned Layla to the edge of the stage and held out a stack of papers to her. Once again, she shook her head vigorously, but the principal was insistent.\par
Layla went back behind the flower bush and frantically leafed through a few of the pages. Then she cleared her throat and began speaking the flower fairy\rquote s lines. \ldblquote Wow\'85 L-look\'85 at\'85 th-the\'85 pretty\'85 f- flowers\'85 everyone!\rdblquote  she said in a shaky voice.\par
The whispers among audience members quieted down. However, things didn\rquote t seem quite under control. Rather, the audience seemed to have grown silent out of even greater shock than before.\par
\ldblquote N-no\'85 n-no\'85 d-don\rquote t\'85 pick\'85 the\'85 f- flowers. You\rquote ll h-hurt them!\rdblquote\par
