Kyle opened his mailbox with the same nervous anticipation as always. He was excited to see a couple of envelopes inside, but this excitement gave way to disappointment when he saw who they were from. He had been sending a letter to Layla every week, but so far, she had not sent him a single response.\par
He let out a quiet sigh, then slumped back up the dormitory\rquote s steps. His room was on the east end of the third floor. With winter break fast approaching, the dormitory was full of students busily packing in preparation for returning home, but Kyle\rquote s room was as quiet as ever.\par
He entered the dark room and tossed the envelopes down on the table by the door. There was an invitation to a social club and a letter from his parents. He left them unopened and threw himself down onto his bed. A sliver of\par
\par
moonlight passed through a gap in the curtains and fell obliquely across his body.\par
\ldblquote Have you considered going on a trip during your first break?\rdblquote  his father had gently suggested to him on a phone call a few days earlier.\par
Many of the wealthier students in the capital traveled all over the continent to broaden their horizons during breaks. Of course. Dr. Ettman had another reason for suggesting this.\par
Kyle had replied that he would think about it. Even though he knew it was pointless to fantasize about going back to Karlsbar and seeing Layla again, it felt just as pointless to go on a trip. He felt more inclined to stay holed up in his room studying, just as he had done all semester long.\par
He closed his eyes and immediately recalled Layla smiling sadly and saying, \ldblquote There is no place in this world where we can be happy together, just the two of us.\rdblquote He also recalled himself at that moment, feeling hopeless and unable to say anything in response.\par
As always, recalling this moment filled him with self-loathing. He hated himself for being so weak, for being nothing without his parents, and especially for having failed to protect the one he loved.\par
Once I become a doctor, he thought, maybe I can find a place for us to be happy together. But that day is still so far in the future. How will I endure all those years without you? Even just these last few months apart have felt like an eternity.\par
He lowered the arm with which he had been covering his eyes and looked at the ceiling. Suddenly it occurred to him that, now that he was legally an adult, he was free to use the money his grandfather had left him as he pleased. He wondered why he hadn\rquote t thought of this before. He sat up and swept back his disheveled hair.\par
\par
After staring into space for a moment, he got up and went to his desk.\par
Opening a drawer, he hurriedly retrieved a pen and a piece of stationery.\par
