Matthias turned up the volume on the phonograph and turned around. All the lights were out in his room; the only illumination was the glow coming from the fireplace.\par
He sat on the warm easy chair next to the fireplace and looked across the room at his bird, which was perched on the armrest of the sofa. As soon as he did, it flew to him. He started whistling the melody of the waltz that was playing on the phonograph, and the canary started singing as if in response.\par
It was a smart bird. Without any training, one day, it started singing along with the music Matthias was listening to. After this, he had made a regular practice of listening to music with the bird. They mainly listened to waltzes. Occasionally he would put on a symphony or a piano piece, but his bird seemed to sing most beautifully when a waltz was playing.\par
The waltz came to an end, and the room was once again full of the sound of the fire crackling.\par
Matthias praised the bird\rquote s singing by petting its little head with the tip of his finger. The bird was used to his touch, and it started lovingly rubbing its beak against his hand.\par
He chuckled, then walked with the bird perched on his finger over to its cage. When he lowered his hand, the bird readily hopped in the cage and settled into its nest.\par
\par
Matthias closed and latched the door of the golden cage, then pulled the needle off the record, which had started playing the next piece. As he lay down in his bed and looked at the ceiling, a long, tired yawn escaped his mouth. Turning to the bird, he said, \ldblquote Sleep well.\rdblquote\par
As if it understood what he had said, the canary went to sleep in its cozy nest.\par
As he gazed at the bird, he was no longer smiling.\par
