As the person who first brought the little girl named Layla Llewellyn to Arvis, the mailman knew better than anyone how much Bill\rquote s life had changed\par
\par
since her arrival. He also knew that this change was the result of Bill\rquote s love for the girl, and it had resulted in him becoming a much happier person than he had been before.\par
After the mailman left, Bill took the letter into the cottage. Layla was at work, so the cottage was empty.\par
Bill opened the drawer where he had been storing the letters from Kyle and was about to put the new one in, but then he impulsively changed his mind and pulled out the whole stack of letters. He knew that Layla would be disappointed and upset with him when he told her. He wasn\rquote t looking forward to that, but he couldn\rquote t stand to keep up this ruse any longer. He tied the letters together with a piece of twine and put them on the dining table.\par
He planned to tell her that evening. He figured that if the letters were already on the table, it would prevent him from changing his mind about telling her. Having made this decision, he already felt like a weight had been lifted. He knew it was right to let Layla make up her own mind about how to deal with the situation, and he intended to support her choice, whatever it was. Thinking forward to the evening\rquote s dinner, he looked in the chicken coop and chose the one he wanted to eat\emdash a nice, plump one. Then he set off towards the mansion\rquote s greenhouse. After tending to the flowerbeds, he was about to enter the storage area next to the greenhouse where he stored\par
firewood when he heard a familiar voice shouting at him.\par
\ldblquote Hey, Bill!\rdblquote  It was the Herhardts\rquote  bird keeper, a friend and coworker that Bill had known for many years. Hurrying towards Bill, he cheerfully said, \ldblquote The butler requested that you please stop stacking up firewood next to the generator in the storage area.\rdblquote\par
Bill frowned. \ldblquote I guess that new-fangled monster takes precedence over me now, huh?\rdblquote\par
\par
A generator had been recently installed in the storage area to provide the mansion with a strange new substance called \ldblquote electricity.\rdblquote  Bill had been informed that the generator made electricity, but he barely even understood what electricity was or what the point of it was. To him, the generator was nothing but a big, annoying contraption that made an awful noise.\par
\ldblquote Fine,\rdblquote  he grumbled, \ldblquote tell him not to worry, I\rquote ll take care of it.\rdblquote  He walked into the storage area, and immediately his eardrums were assaulted by the loud din of the generator. \ldblquote You stupid thing!\rdblquote  he said, glaring at the machine like it was his enemy. \ldblquote That noise you make is still just as irritating as the first day I heard it.\rdblquote\par
Bill\rquote s disdain for all the flashy new products of the modern age was yet another thing he had in common with Dowager Herhardt.\par
Thinking bitterly of how quiet the storage area had used to be before electricity, he started stacking up the firewood even closer to the giant device. He figured that if he constructed a thick enough wall, it would block some of the noise and make it easier for him to do his work.\par
\ldblquote That darn thing seems even louder today than usual,\rdblquote  he muttered, shaking his head.\par
He thought of something he had heard Dowager Herhardt say many times: \ldblquote Things used to be so much nicer. \rdblquote He agreed with this sentiment today more than ever.\par
