The children\rquote s attitudes about the upcoming winter break were sharply divided. Some were practically leaping for joy, while others were sad and depressed.\par
Among those in the second group, Monica was the saddest of all. She left tear stains on Layla\rquote s skirt before finally leaving. Layla waved to the little girl as she slowly walked off with her parents, looking over her shoulder repeatedly and sniffling. It pained Layla\rquote s heart to think of how sad Monica would surely be when she found out her teacher was leaving for a different school.\par
I\rquote d better find a time to bid all the kids farewell before I transfer schools, she resolved as she diligently packed up her things.\par
To have enough time to roast a chicken, she would need to start dinner preparations earlier than normal. On her way home, she needed to buy some thick winter socks and a sweater for Bill, and she also planned to get a nice bottle of wine for them to share. She hoped this would soften the blow when she told him that she was going to be working in a different city from the next semester onward. She decided to get a bottle of liquor as well, just for good measure.\par
As she took off her indoor shoes and put on her outdoor ones, she snickered, remembering a silly comment the duke had made the night of the benefit performance: \ldblquote Do you really think that will keep me from taking them?\rdblquote\par
\par
She had been quite surprised by the comment. She hadn\rquote t realized that he was capable of that sort of playful banter.\par
Lately, he hadn\rquote t been tormenting her like in the past. She wondered if it was because everything had quieted down for winter. At first, she had been suspicious, worrying that he was plotting something, but gradually she had relaxed. She wondered why his behavior had shifted, but she didn\rquote t feel like pondering over it. He had probably just lost interest in her. She certainly hoped that this was the case.\par
After buttoning up her winter coat, she headed out of the school into the cold. She put the box of her things in her bicycle\rquote s basket and started riding towards the city, going faster than usual. Since her basket was full, the things she bought had to be tied to the bicycle\rquote s back seat.\par
