The night train from the capital arrived at Karlsbar Central Station. The sun hadn\rquote t yet risen on this winter morning, so the platform was suffused with twilight.\par
Having just awoken, the passengers were bleary-eyed, but they hurried to get off the train. Soon the platform was crowded with people lugging heavy suitcases.\par
Just as the commotion was starting to die down, Kyle stepped off the train. It was an unannounced visit home, so there was no one there to pick him up. His parents believed him to be on an international train headed to the southern part of the continent. Indeed, Kyle himself had thought that was where he would be headed, until he set foot in Ratse Central Station and changed his mind.\par
After he had gathered all his courage to send the last letter to Layla, she still hadn\rquote t responded. This was a sign that it was time to let go. At least, that\rquote s how he took it at first. That\rquote s why he had followed his father\rquote s advice and decided to broaden his horizons by going on a trip.\par
And yet, he had suddenly changed his mind and boarded a train headed to Karlsbar instead.\par
He pondered over the reason for his sudden change of heart throughout the whole train ride but couldn\rquote t come up with a good answer.\par
\par
All he knew is he had a hunch. It was an ominous hunch, based on the fact that it was not like the Layla he knew to completely cut off all communication with him. He worried that something had happened to her. He was well aware that he could wrong, that she might be fine and his mind might be playing tricks on him. But even if this was the case, he knew that he needed to see her with his own two eyes. That was all he wanted.\par
Carrying a large trunk, he plodded across the platform, from which the rest of the crowd was already gone. Unlike the previous summer when he had looked so gaunt and haggard, he now looked like a healthy young man, full of life.\par
He exited the station and saw the square covered in snow. His head and shoulders were soon coated with white powder as the flakes continued their slow, silent dance.\par
\ldblquote Layla\'85\rdblquote  he whispered, the name turning into white steam as it left his mouth. He felt a dull ache in the pit of his stomach, and his heart started to increase its pace. Lifting his heavy trunk, he strode across the snowy square.\par
I will cherish you. You will be the whole world to me. I won\rquote t let you get hurt, Layla.\par
These words from the last letter he had sent expressed his deepest feelings. All she had to do was give him the slightest nod, and he was ready to keep his promise.\par
I\rquote ll come get you. Let\rquote s go to a place where we can be happy together.\par
