clearer and more awake her mind had become.\par
Suddenly a strong wind rattled the window. Startled, she turned and looked. For a moment, she thought that maybe Phoebe had brought a note to the window, even though she knew she had carefully locked her cage. She reminded herself that Phoebe no longer paid any visits to the Ettman residence, and the duke was out of town.\par
Feeling relieved after thinking this through, she got out of bed and draped a shawl over her shoulders. She didn\rquote t have anything in particular that she was planning to do, she just couldn\rquote t stand the uneasy stillness in her room.\par
\par
Unpleasant memories of her lonesome childhood kept popping into her head. All the loneliness, sadness, and fear that she had felt back then seemed to have been rekindled recently.\par
She paced around her room for a while, then went to the kitchen and drank a glass of water. However, she still felt thirsty, and her lips started quivering. To try to calm herself down, she calculated how long it was going to be until Uncle Bill returned, but this ended up only making her feel more lonely.\par
This is ridiculous, she thought. Uncle Bill has only been gone a day or two. Why is the fact that I\rquote m alone affecting me so much right now?\par
She double-checked that the door and windows were locked, then made a cup of hot tea and sat down at the kitchen table.\par
As the turbulent wind howled outside, she thought she could hear Claudine\rquote s voice saying, \ldblquote Poor thing.\rdblquote\par
With each sip that she took of the hot tea, she felt that she was only getting colder. Eventually, she helplessly set the mug down on the table.\par
She wished that kicking sticks and stones on the path as she walked home could have let her forget her emotions like it had used to. On her way back from the mansion, she had simply kept her head down, looking at the shadow she was casting in the moonlight.\par
She felt guilty and embarrassed. No matter what Claudine did to insult or hurt her, she felt like she didn\rquote t have the right to get upset. She had stolen Claudine\rquote s fiance. It didn\rquote t matter that she hadn\rquote t wanted to. She had done it all the same.\par
