Mark Evers felt slightly uneasy as he followed his master. Having started out as an ordinary servant before rising to the position of assistant to\par
the duke, he had known Duke Herhardt for a long time, but he had never seen this behavior from him before. He seemed very distracted, even though there was a very important banquet for the crown prince and princess coming up. His mind seemed to be in a different place.\par
When the duke came to a sudden stop. Mark stopped as well. The duke was looking out a window halfway down the hallway. The sun was setting. The window looked out onto the forest path behind the mansion\rquote s garden.\par
There was someone walking down this path. It was a small-framed woman, and she was carrying something in her arms. It was Layla.\par
Once he had figured this out. Mark didn\rquote t hurry his master along, but just waited quietly. Matthias stayed at the window for a long while, silently gazing\par
\par
at the view outside. Layla came back, he thought to himself.\par
She seemed to have gone to the market. She had a large, brown-paper bag in her arms. It looked very heavy, but she was walking with her usual, brisk gait, with her head held high. Layla came back. As this fact was sinking in for him, his stiff shoulders relaxed.\par
Layla continued down the path, not looking back once. Her French braid swayed with each step. He looked at the hems of her coat and skirt flapping in the wind, and her scrawny legs underneath them. Then he looked again at her hair swaying on her back. Looking closer, he noticed that her shoulders were scrunched up as she walked. It was then that he realized how cold it was outside.\par
There was a bus that ran all the way to the village entrance, but on days when Layla didn\rquote t ride her bicycle, she preferred to walk instead of riding the bus. This seemed to be the case today as well. Even while carrying that heavy load in this frigid weather.\par
As she walked into the distance, Matthias turned from the window and continued walking down the hallway\rquote s red carpet, to resume his designated life. However, the image of Layla walking alone down that cold, dusky path stuck in his mind for a long time. The brighter the lights inside the mansion shone, the more vivid the memory grew in his mind. The unidentifiable emotion that this memory was evoking in him felt like a thorn stuck in his throat.\par
As soon as he stepped into the drawing room, which was full of guests who would be attending the banquet, Claudine rushed over to greet him. \ldblquote Duke Herhardt, you\rquote re here at last!\rdblquote  she exclaimed, casually linking arms with him.\par
One by one, the guests started to look at the couple.\par
\par
\ldblquote I was worried that something had happened,\rdblquote  Claudine said. \rdblquote I was about to go check on you. You\rquote re never late like this\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I just got a little delayed in my preparations for the banquet,\rdblquote  he replied. \ldblquote Oh, I see. Well, that\rquote s a relief,\rdblquote  she replied, maintaining a relaxed smile\par
even while so many people were looking at her.\par
She then rejoined the convivial atmosphere in the drawing room with perfect decorum. It was clear that she was going to make an excellent lady of the house. Matthias recognized this as well. And yet, for some reason, all of a sudden, Layla\rquote s face popped into his mind.\par
After watching Claudine chatting with his grandmother, mother, and the crown princess, he slowly shifted his gaze to the blazing sire in the fireplace across the room.\par
He still knew what a perfect marriage was. It was a marriage that would serve as a stepping stone to stabilize his world, without involving needless emotions. That was the type of marriage he had been taught to want, and it was the type he intended to get. Claudine Brandt was the perfect partner for such a marriage. He was well aware of this. But will she be enough for me? he suddenly wondered.\par
Just as Matthias was lifting his narrowed eyes to look at the chandelier, Hessen entered to announce that the banquet was about to begin. Everyone stood up and headed with their companions to the banquet hall.\par
Matthias escorted Claudine down the stairs. The generator had been repaired just in time, so the large chandelier in the banquet hall, one of Arvis\rquote s sources of pride, was on full display.\par
Underneath, the lavishly decorated banquet table awaited the guests.\par
Before sitting in his designated seat, Matthias looked at the mural on the high ceiling, the chandelier, and the wall covered with portraits.\par
\par
As the lord of Arvis, all of this was his. And yet, his woman wasn\rquote t here. With her slender shoulders hunched, she was walking down a cold forest path towards an old, dilapidated cottage. Looking at the dazzling banquet table in front of him, he imagined Layla sitting alone at a shabby kitchen table.\par
Just then, Claudine called out to him. \ldblquote Duke Herhardt?\rdblquote\par
He looked at her, and she glanced at the chair in front of her.\par
He understood that she was waiting for him to pull the chair out from the table for her, but he didn\rquote t oblige right away. Instead, he just stood for a moment, looking at his fiancee. He had known her since they were children, and she would soon be his wife, but suddenly it felt like he was looking at a stranger. It was an odd feeling.\par
\ldblquote Duke Herhardt?\rdblquote  Claudine said again, frowning slightly.\par
He apologized by giving a slight nod, then casually pulled out the chair for her. Relieved that he was acting like himself again, Claudine regained her smile and sat down.\par
It was the start of a magnificent banquet.\par
