All the meetings for Matthias\rquote s business trip, which had been expected to take at least four days, were wrapped up after only three days.\par
Leaning back into a chair in the hotel lobby. Mark Evers let out an exhausted sigh. He had worked without rest for the previous three days, contacting people and moving meetings around. This lunch meeting was the last piece of official business on the trip.\par
Mark lifted his tired face and stared out the window. A deluge of invitations from the city\rquote s most prominent businessmen and aristocrats had come in, but the duke had made sure that there were no meetings scheduled for the final day of his trip. It didn\rquote t take Mark long to realize what the duke intended to spend the free time on: Layla.\par
That morning, the duke had summoned Mark and told him to send a car to Arvis to pick up Layla and bring her to him. That was the extent of the\par
\par
duke\rquote s command, but Mark also understood why the duke wanted Layla brought to him. Looking at his watch, he realized that the car was probably just about to arrive at Arvis.\par
Sometimes he wondered how much longer it would be possible to keep the duke\rquote s affair a secret. No matter how simple-minded Bill Remmer was, it seemed only a matter of time before he would figure out that something untoward was going on with his beloved adopted daughter. The same went for the duke\rquote s mother and grandmother, and for Lady Claudine.\par
However, he felt silly for worrying about this when he considered the way the duke had been behaving recently. Basically, the duke had been acting like he didn\rquote t care about hiding the affair at all. Granted, it wasn\rquote t uncommon for men to be seen in public with their mistresses, but it was a different matter when the man was as prominent a figure as Duke Herhardt. He couldn\rquote t believe the duke was so blithely engaging in behavior that could damage his spotless reputation. It was something the Duke Herhardt he had come to know would have never done.\par
Of course, it wasn\rquote t the duke that he was really worried about. The person who would be hurt most if a scandal were to break out was Layla. If he longer intends to keep the affair a secret, what\rquote s his plan for her? he wondered.\par
His eyes wandered around the hotel lobby in which he was sitting, traveling from the ornate, gold- plated pillars, to the marble statues, before settling on the rows of potted plants, which had fresh green leaves even though it was the dead of winter.\par
Mark was not close friends with Bill Remmer, who was old enough to be his father, but he knew that the gardener was well-liked by most everyone among Arvis\rquote s staff, as was Layla.\par
\par
The House of Herhardt\rquote s staff members often referred to Layla as Arvis\rquote s child. Perhaps it was a bombastic way to describe an orphan girl who had been taken in and raised by the estate\rquote s gardener, but somehow it felt natural to everyone. And this was true even though she was not the only child raised by a member of Arvis\rquote s staff.\par
Reflecting on this, Mark wondered if it was because no other staff member\rquote s child was as universally beloved as Layla was. Throughout her childhood, everyone saw her frolicking through the estate\rquote s garden, forest, and riverside. When you encountered her running around the flowery garden, or the lush forest, it was like a fairy was brushing past you.\par
Everyone had expected her to marry someone nice, like Kyle, and live happily ever after. But it seemed that things weren\rquote t turning out so well for her. Of course, by helping the duke. Mark was partly to blame for this, so he felt silly for pitying her.\par
He remembered the weighty advice Hessen had given him on the day he was appointed to the position of the duke\rquote s assistant: \ldblquote Remember, Evers, It\rquote s not our place to judge.\rdblquote\par
Mark understood exactly what this meant. He was to carry out the duke\rquote s will by following whatever orders he was given. Anything beyond that would be overstepping his authority.\par
As he finished reflecting on this, a group of men walked out into the lobby.\par
It seemed that the lunch had concluded.\par
He quickly stood up and rushed to the duke\rquote s side. From a distance, no one would have been able to tell that the duke had just concluded three days of back-to-back meetings\emdash he looked impeccable. Up close, however, Mark could see the fatigue on his face.\par
\par
\ldblquote The car left before lunch,\rdblquote  Mark said. \ldblquote It should arrive back here around teatime.\rdblquote\par
The duke nodded. Mark noticed there were bags under his eyes.\par
\ldblquote Once the car arrives, I will bring her to your room. Until then, perhaps you would like to get some rest, Your Grace?\rdblquote  Mark cautiously asked.\par
\ldblquote Sure,\rdblquote  the duke replied. After bidding his lunch companions farewell, he went to his room.\par
As usual, Mark followed a few steps behind him. He was sure that the duke was exhausted, but he noticed that he was walking with perfect posture, and a relaxed, dignified gait.\par
The various thoughts he had been having about Layla were making Mark uneasy. Duke Herhardt was just Duke Herhardt. That name and title were infused into every aspect of his life. It saddened Mark to contemplate what someone like Layla Llewellyn meant to a man like that.\par
\ldblquote Ah, Evers,\rdblquote  the duke said as they arrived at the door of his room, \ldblquote I have one more request for you.\rdblquote\par
